{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfRc7B5gZmBdQ6scTMp3S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 05 is done. Everything is written, tested and verified; nothing was committed and nothing touched `solutions/`.\n\n**Answer to §2.9: no, the matrix is not part of the hardness story.** With customer labels fixed, re-covering the same edge set with different cliques leaves the default node count to refute `optimum − 1` exactly unchanged in every one of 15,900 pairs. Graph-only features predict nodes as well as the full set, so the kill criterion is met.\n\nWhat was measured, all at 10 ≤ n ≤ 40:\n\n- **Re-coverings.** 1,400 campaign bases, 15,900 applicable re-coverings by split, merge and greedy edge-clique cover, in 12,031 matrix classes. Every one kept the base's neighbour masks and nauty certificate, solved independently to the same optimum, and matched the base's default node count exactly.\n- **The one matrix channel.** Under `csearch`, 394 pairs differ, all among the 622 where the re-covering crossed the `better_move` density threshold. Theorem 2 saves 10–13% of nodes at the median for n ≥ 25, up to 25%, and never costs a node. That is a configuration rule, not a property of the problem.\n- **Labelling matters more than the matrix.** Renaming customers of the same matrix changes the default count on 588 of 800 bases at n ≥ 25, by 2–6% at the median and up to 2.3×, up to 8× under `csearch`. This is the floor for any graph-invariant predictor.\n- **§8's counts.** `count_search` is equal in all 4,516 re-covered pairs at n ≤ 15. `count_closing` differs in nearly every greedy pair, by 2.9–4.5× at the median and up to 1,540×.\n- **Corpus pairs are uninformative.** The 150 graph classes with several matrix classes are near-complete graphs refuted in 0–13 nodes, and the report says so.\n- **Kill test.** Graph-only MAE 0.0948 against full 0.0968 in log10 nodes on the deduplicated campaign, matrix-only 25–30% worse, grouped by graph class.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- `learning/graph_story.py` with the constructions, the study and the tables. `reports/ml_nature.md` §13 and `reports/graph_story_tables.md`.\n- `tests/test_graph_story.py`, seven tests including a hand-checked instance. Full suite: 805 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed.\n- Artifact: `recover.csv.gz`, `relabel.csv.gz`, `recover_lattice.csv` under `learning/data/ensemble/`, about 2 MB. The 64 MB of re-covered witnesses are git-ignored since they regenerate in three minutes from the CSV rows.\n- PROGRESS.md has the iteration 5 section and reads 5/6 solved. Item 05 is marked done in iterations.md. Item 06 remains, so no LOOP_COMPLETE line.\n\nOne note for item 06: hardness is a function of the labelled graph, so the 125×125 node predictor needs graph features plus a label-noise term, and the recertify counts run under `better_move=True`, whose expected saving §13's flip table now quantifies."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2319,"cache_read_input_tokens":177800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2319,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9aef81d3-2737-4d16-adb0-5a4195051fd9","uuid":"ccdfff4b-e45a-4d7a-a3d3-02fce16fe380","timestamp":"2026-09-26T06:50:21.282Z","request_id":"req_011CfRc7ARVW981vfxzcFnQo"}
